How often have you had an experience that seems a little too coincidental? What was it? Did it feel meaningful for you? How did it reflect what life was like for you in that moment? 

	i’m willing to guess something like this has happened to you before: You’re consuming media of some kind (watching TV, a movie, a video online, maybe even reading a book!) and you learn an interesting fact that sticks with you. After you learn about it, it seems to come up in your daily life often. You start to see information related to that thing in other places besides where you learned about it, you might hear it in songs or podcasts, maybe you overhear a conversation that includes a reference to it. It seems like this little bit of information is making the rounds. What is this all about?

People with a scientific understanding of brain and mind (CIII) would call these events a frequency illusion. Described as a kind of cognitive bias, the mind has taken in a piece of information that’s novel or interesting. As a result of the chemical reactions that the brain produces when such a piece of information is learned, said brain then dedicates more background resources into searching for that information elsewhere. The mechanism is one that’s been developed over time through evolution: novel information could potentially save the life of the person who learned it. Self-protection is a major function of the mind. It makes total sense that learning something that spikes a chemical reaction becomes more important to the brain. There could be an instance in the future where recalling that information means the difference between life and death.

The development of such a mechanism (and all other mechanisms of body:brain:mind) occurred over thousands of years and has been passed down through generations via DNA and RNA. DNA is the storage system of all accumulated knowledge of experience gathered by a lineage of people through both direct and indirect experience. The experiences of previous family members stretching back to the very beginnings of humanity’s earliest forms is kept safe in DNA. RNA, meanwhile, is the communication system that interprets that information. RNA sends signals to create individual proteins that match the information given by the experiences that have been stored. If DNA is the “blueprint” then RNA is the construction crew. When combined, these two molecules make up the entirety of a human being. The way one looks, when one begins to crawl:stand:walk, the development of their personality, their intelligence, all of the things that make a human a human are kept within DNA and RNA.

Have you ever considered that, in their own way, both DNA and RNA are themselves a kind of intelligence? They may not communicate in a way that we as the human beings in which those molecules inhabit can understand but they do carry information and send that information outward into complex systems. How else might someone describe intelligence? Applied knowledge. When learned information is turned into an action or actions, that knowledge as applied then becomes whatever the intended result might be. Only through the application of knowledge do intelligent systems emerge. 

What else is a human being but a living:holistic:intelligent system? With the constant writing and building going on between the DNA:RNA exchange, the totality of our physical inner lives with the myriad organisms we host inside of us that keep us alive, and our own desires that we act out, we are applying the knowledge we’ve gathered since our bloodline began along with everything that has affected our “blueprints” since, including our own personal experiences. It’s through the realization that we are whole systems unto ourselves that we start to wonder if we’re more than that. From the first instances of religious thought, that wondering had been filled by all things Circuit V. But we’re learning to think and live as a gestalt now. If that’s true, isn’t it likely there’s more than some being or beings that oversee our every move?

