When Circuit VI is active, the mind can find many ways of operating as part of a gestalt. i believe that is this Circuits ultimate function: to keep someone who experiences its effects regularly primed to keep experiencing it. The way i found to accomplish this is through the adoption and regular research of Quantum Mechanics (QM). Plenty has been said about the impact quantum mechanics has had on humanity over the last 120 years. From humble beginnings in Helgoland to the latest innovations the world over, QM has steadily risen to the top as the science of the moment. 

With research in the applications of QM on computers becoming the new normal, it’s only a matter of time before an openly available quantum operating system for computers is built. At the moment, the most viable application of QM on current computer systems is the creation of true randomization. Currently all “random” computer systems are limited by programming. There’s nothing truly random about them aside from the parameters set by the mathematics that run them. A truly random system would utilize quantum superposition to detect particles that jump from one orbit around an atom to the next. 

∞ Superposition is the function of particles in orbit around an atom that states there is no way to determine a particle’s location in any specific orbit without first measuring the particle somehow. Observing:measuring a particle causes collapse, which is a temporary state a particle takes that determines not only the orbit it’s in but what specific measurement is being made. Particles around atoms not only follow an orbit, but they have specific speeds and rotations of their own. Currently, measurements can only be done to one of these factors. It’s impossible to try measuring more than one aspect of a particle at the same time as collapse is only fractions of a second long.

∞ Further, collapse is part of what’s known as the measurement problem, a greater debate within QM that causes a divide between the professionals that study it. Some argue that collapse and superposition are strictly mathematical equations that were developed to aid in observation. Others say that human consciousness has an effect on matter in a way that changes physical reality, that a person’s thoughts change the outcome of measurements based on internal biases. One of the more telling aspects of observation is the change of a particle from being observed either as a particle or as a wave. Particles that aren’t viewed by a consciousness commonly remain as particles (say, when they’re purposely fired toward a barrier). When observed however, a wholly different visual pattern emerges. Particles change from being visually observed as dots against a barrier to long, parallel lines with spaces between them. Until recently, these dot and line patterns had never been seen together. However, as of April 8, 2024, a team of researchers from both Sarbonne Universite and FERMI have built an apparatus that can apparently observe both waves and particles at once. Waves are detected as large blotches instead of single, small dots. This unprecedented experiment is paving a future where control of observation may be possible in time.

Using superposition in a more spiritual approach to reality offers a plethora of choices to me. i’ve adopted the term stochastic to define the randomization of choices available to me at any given moment. i’ve heard scientists warn not to conflate stochastic processes with randomization, but i don’t believe my life choices to be random in that sense. When i have options for action in my life, they all come to me in a series of flashes of insight. To me, doing something that aligns with a stochastic process is to do the least likely of all of those options (as long as it doesn’t harm myself or others) in order to affect my current ‘verse:timeline. Scientifically, a stochastic process is one where a process has properties that are randomly distributed where a pattern might emerge, allowing for statistical analysis but no precise predictions about the results. Have you ever seen a graph where dozens of dots are dispersed on an X/Y plane and a solid line runs through the center of the dots to give an average result? That’s a stochastic process.

It’s through my own definition of superposition and stochastic process within my gnosis that i change my life through my thoughts, words, and actions. When i make choices that feel challenging:difficult for me, i know that i’m moving in a direction of change for my internal self and my life. i’ve learned to embrace the weird. i’m learning to be more bold. Risks are the only way we gain rewards. My reward for being present is sometimes getting presents for it. Presents don’t have to be a physical object i can take with me. i do my best to disengage from expecting such things (i’ve found that tends to happen without any intent). Instead, i like to think of smiles from strangers, sudden rays of sunshine, and other small things to be part of the gifts i’m open to receiving. Remaining open in this way can change my mood for the positive. It’s an active practice but one that is more fun when i’m fully invested and engaged with my life. It’s rare that i disengage from that mindset. That usually only happens when i’m under serious distress.

